This is super cool and has direct implications for AI visibility.
If LLM prototype rankings diverge from human rankings (0.15 correlation is basically noise), then being "obviously" in a category isn't enough. The model's idea of "typical" isn't yours.
The atypical-member finding is the key.... brands that explicitly state their category membership in content embed closer to the category than brands that just ARE the category and never say it. "Loganix is a link building agency" appearing across sources > just being one and assuming AI knows.
Extends what we're seeing in retrieval architecture research (McGill NLP, LLM2Vec-Gen).... the systems aren't preserving human-intuitive category structure....they're compressing it. Which means the fight isn't to be the "best" in a category. It's to be IN the category at all, then win on what retrieval can actually distinguish.
